    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a near-exact structural mirror of the already-established `ValidateOwnerPostcode` / `InvalidPostcodeException` pattern, and everything lands in the right unit:\n\n- **Function step** \u2014 `ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate` matches its sibling `ValidateOwnerPostcode` precisely: same package, `@Val OwnerFieldsDto request`, the early-return-when-null guard, a single public `service` method (the codebase convention \u2014 both existing validators use `service`, not the `validate` of the AGENTS.md example), throwing a checked escalation. Validation is correctly its own step rather than being folded into `ValidateOwner` or `BuildOwner`.\n- **Exception** \u2014 `FutureRegistrationDateException extends Exception` with a message-building constructor, consistent with the other escalation exceptions; the Javadoc cross-links the handler and `BusinessDays` for context.\n- **Handler** \u2014 byte-for-byte the shape of `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build` helper rather than duplicating ProblemDetail assembly.\n- **Escalation YAML** \u2014 same two-line form as the existing escalation files.\n- **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted right after `validate` (which publishes the `Out<OwnerFieldsDto>` this step consumes) and before `validatePostcode`, carrying `govern: [ transaction ]` like every other step. Ordering is correct: it runs after the body is published and alongside the other body validations, before `build`.\n\nNo duplication \u2014 the future-date check is a distinct concern from `BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate` (defaulting/weekend-rolling), and keeping the throwing validation out of that static helper preserves its single-responsibility role. No class or method is becoming a catch-all. A maintainer would find this entirely natural.",
